1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,138
Ninety-three million miles
away, our star is restless.

2
00:00:10,218 --> 00:00:16,387
Beneath its glowing surface, twisted
magnetic fields store unimaginable energy.

3
00:00:16,467 --> 00:00:18,941
And when those fields finally snap,

4
00:00:19,021 --> 00:00:23,843
the Sun hurls billions of tons
of superheated gas into space.

5
00:00:23,923 --> 00:00:26,139
A solar storm is born.

6
00:00:26,289 --> 00:00:30,485
Within days, it can reach Earth,
painting our skies with light,

7
00:00:30,565 --> 00:00:34,668
and testing the technology
our modern world depends on.

8
00:00:34,748 --> 00:00:37,944
This is the story of those
storms, of how they form,

9
00:00:38,024 --> 00:00:42,090
how they travel, and how we
have learned to watch them.

10
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:47,082
The Sun holds more than
ninety-nine percent

11
00:00:47,162 --> 00:00:49,811
of all the mass in our solar system.

12
00:00:49,891 --> 00:00:53,834
Deep in its core, at fifteen
million degrees Celsius,

13
00:00:53,914 --> 00:00:59,598
hydrogen fuses into helium,
releasing the energy that lights our days.

14
00:00:59,678 --> 00:01:05,177
That energy takes many thousands of
years to work its way out to the surface.

15
00:01:05,257 --> 00:01:10,154
Then, as light, it crosses the gap
to Earth in just over eight minutes.

16
00:01:10,234 --> 00:01:15,117
Its visible surface glows at about
five and a half thousand degrees.

17
00:01:15,197 --> 00:01:18,512
Yet above it, the thin
outer atmosphere called the

18
00:01:18,592 --> 00:01:22,561
corona is far hotter,
at more than a million degrees.

19
00:01:22,641 --> 00:01:25,343
Exactly how the corona is heated remains

20
00:01:25,423 --> 00:01:28,853
one of the great mysteries
of solar science.

21
00:01:28,933 --> 00:01:31,835
But the Sun is not a calm, solid ball.

22
00:01:31,915 --> 00:01:35,050
It is made of plasma,
gas so hot that its atoms

23
00:01:35,130 --> 00:01:38,510
have been torn apart
into charged particles.

24
00:01:38,590 --> 00:01:42,667
And charged particles in
motion create magnetic fields.

25
00:01:42,747 --> 00:01:47,551
The Sun also spins faster at
its equator than near its poles.

26
00:01:47,631 --> 00:01:52,992
Over time, this uneven rotation stretches
and twists the magnetic field lines,

27
00:01:53,072 --> 00:01:56,511
like rubber bands wound
tighter and tighter.

28
00:01:56,591 --> 00:02:02,284
Where these tangled fields push up through
the surface, they leave a mark we can see.

29
00:02:04,134 --> 00:02:07,955
Sunspots look like dark
blemishes on the face of the Sun.

30
00:02:08,035 --> 00:02:10,440
They are regions where intense magnetic

31
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:13,758
fields hold back the
flow of heat from below.

32
00:02:13,838 --> 00:02:18,452
At around four thousand degrees,
they are cooler than their surroundings,

33
00:02:18,532 --> 00:02:21,474
so they appear dark,
yet they would still blaze

34
00:02:21,554 --> 00:02:24,946
brighter than a full moon
if seen on their own.

35
00:02:25,026 --> 00:02:28,626
Many sunspots are larger
than the entire Earth.

36
00:02:28,706 --> 00:02:32,768
Their numbers rise and fall in
a rhythm called the solar cycle,

37
00:02:32,848 --> 00:02:35,256
which lasts about eleven years.

38
00:02:35,336 --> 00:02:39,718
At solar minimum, the Sun can
go weeks without a single spot.

39
00:02:39,798 --> 00:02:43,018
At solar maximum,
it can be covered with them,

40
00:02:43,098 --> 00:02:47,628
and its entire magnetic field
flips, north becoming south.

41
00:02:47,708 --> 00:02:51,005
In 2024, scientists
announced that the Sun had

42
00:02:51,085 --> 00:02:54,906
entered the maximum phase
of its current cycle.

43
00:02:54,986 --> 00:03:00,853
And the more active the Sun becomes,
the more often its magnetic fields erupt.

44
00:03:02,703 --> 00:03:06,931
When twisted magnetic field lines
suddenly snap and reconnect,

45
00:03:07,011 --> 00:03:10,647
they release their stored
energy in an instant.

46
00:03:10,727 --> 00:03:14,325
The result is a solar flare,
an intense flash of

47
00:03:14,405 --> 00:03:18,913
light across the spectrum,
from radio waves to X-rays.

48
00:03:18,993 --> 00:03:22,116
A large flare can
release as much energy as

49
00:03:22,196 --> 00:03:25,918
millions of hydrogen
bombs exploding at once.

50
00:03:25,998 --> 00:03:28,693
Scientists rank flares by their strength,

51
00:03:28,773 --> 00:03:32,323
with the most powerful
known as X-class flares.

52
00:03:32,403 --> 00:03:37,509
The largest flare ever measured
erupted in November 2003.

53
00:03:37,589 --> 00:03:42,564
It was so intense that it overwhelmed
the instruments built to record it.

54
00:03:42,644 --> 00:03:47,697
Because a flare is light, its radiation
reaches Earth in about eight minutes,

55
00:03:47,777 --> 00:03:53,479
and it can disrupt radio communication
on the side of the planet facing the Sun.

56
00:03:53,559 --> 00:03:56,854
Often, a flare comes with
something even bigger.

57
00:03:56,934 --> 00:04:00,152
Above the surface,
magnetic fields can also

58
00:04:00,232 --> 00:04:03,143
lift enormous arches of glowing plasma,

59
00:04:03,223 --> 00:04:07,658
called prominences,
that hang in the corona for days.

60
00:04:07,738 --> 00:04:12,560
When their magnetic support breaks,
they too can be flung out into space.

61
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:18,351
A coronal mass ejection, or CME,
is a vast cloud of plasma and magnetic

62
00:04:18,431 --> 00:04:23,940
field blasted away from the Sun's
outer atmosphere, the corona.

63
00:04:24,020 --> 00:04:28,370
A single eruption can carry
a billion tons of material.

64
00:04:28,450 --> 00:04:33,551
The fastest race outward at around
three thousand kilometers per second.

65
00:04:33,631 --> 00:04:38,580
As it expands, a CME can grow
larger than the Sun itself.

66
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:42,500
Most of these eruptions head
off harmlessly into space.

67
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:45,853
But sometimes, one is
aimed straight at us.

68
00:04:48,203 --> 00:04:51,594
Even on quiet days,
the Sun constantly breathes out

69
00:04:51,674 --> 00:04:55,582
a stream of charged particles
called the solar wind.

70
00:04:55,662 --> 00:04:59,448
It flows past Earth at nearly
a million miles per hour.

71
00:04:59,528 --> 00:05:03,308
A coronal mass ejection is
like a gust inside that wind,

72
00:05:03,388 --> 00:05:06,239
a shock front plowing through space.

73
00:05:06,319 --> 00:05:10,839
Depending on its speed, it can
cross the hundred and fifty million

74
00:05:10,919 --> 00:05:16,306
kilometers to Earth in as little as
fifteen hours, or take several days.

75
00:05:16,386 --> 00:05:19,401
Fortunately, our planet has a shield.

76
00:05:19,551 --> 00:05:25,186
Earth's molten iron core generates a
magnetic field that surrounds the planet,

77
00:05:25,266 --> 00:05:28,933
a protective bubble
called the magnetosphere.

78
00:05:29,013 --> 00:05:33,168
Most of the time, it deflects
the solar wind around us.

79
00:05:33,248 --> 00:05:38,684
But when a powerful CME strikes,
the magnetosphere is squeezed and shaken.

80
00:05:38,764 --> 00:05:44,391
If the magnetic field of the cloud points
in the opposite direction to Earth's own,

81
00:05:44,471 --> 00:05:49,516
the two can link up, and energy
pours into our magnetic environment.

82
00:05:49,596 --> 00:05:52,248
This is a geomagnetic storm.

83
00:05:52,398 --> 00:05:54,054
On the night side of Earth,

84
00:05:54,134 --> 00:05:57,142
the magnetosphere is
stretched into a long tail,

85
00:05:57,222 --> 00:06:00,293
reaching far beyond the orbit of the Moon.

86
00:06:00,373 --> 00:06:03,071
During a storm, that tail stores energy,

87
00:06:03,151 --> 00:06:08,057
then suddenly snaps back,
hurling particles toward the planet.

88
00:06:09,907 --> 00:06:13,921
The most beautiful sign of
a solar storm is the aurora.

89
00:06:14,001 --> 00:06:17,405
Charged particles guided
by Earth's magnetic field

90
00:06:17,485 --> 00:06:21,408
rain down into the upper
atmosphere near the poles.

91
00:06:21,488 --> 00:06:25,993
There, they collide with oxygen
and nitrogen, making them glow.

92
00:06:26,073 --> 00:06:31,851
Oxygen shines green and red, while
nitrogen adds shades of blue and purple.

93
00:06:31,931 --> 00:06:36,661
Auroras appear around both poles at
the same time, the northern lights

94
00:06:36,741 --> 00:06:41,853
and the southern lights, mirror
images on opposite ends of the world.

95
00:06:41,933 --> 00:06:45,335
Seen from orbit, auroras
ripple across the planet

96
00:06:45,415 --> 00:06:49,339
like living curtains,
hundreds of kilometers tall.

97
00:06:49,419 --> 00:06:54,051
During strong storms, they spread
far beyond the polar regions.

98
00:06:54,131 --> 00:06:58,654
In May 2024, the strongest
geomagnetic storm in more than

99
00:06:58,734 --> 00:07:04,030
twenty years lit up skies as
far south as Florida and Mexico.

100
00:07:04,110 --> 00:07:07,923
Millions of people saw an
aurora for the very first time.

101
00:07:08,003 --> 00:07:13,282
But the same storms that create such
beauty can also cause real damage.

102
00:07:15,632 --> 00:07:17,385
On September 1st, 1859,

103
00:07:17,465 --> 00:07:21,767
the English astronomer Richard
Carrington was sketching

104
00:07:21,847 --> 00:07:28,273
sunspots when he saw two brilliant beads
of white light flare up on the Sun.

105
00:07:28,353 --> 00:07:31,476
It was the first solar
flare ever recorded.

106
00:07:31,556 --> 00:07:34,739
Less than a day later,
the eruption reached Earth.

107
00:07:34,819 --> 00:07:36,907
Auroras glowed over the Caribbean,

108
00:07:36,987 --> 00:07:41,949
so bright that people in some places
could read by their light at night.

109
00:07:42,029 --> 00:07:46,311
Telegraph systems across Europe
and North America failed.

110
00:07:46,391 --> 00:07:51,285
Some operators received electric shocks,
and some lines kept working even

111
00:07:51,365 --> 00:07:56,436
after their batteries were disconnected,
powered by the storm itself.

112
00:07:56,516 --> 00:08:01,335
In 1859, the world had
little technology to lose.

113
00:08:01,415 --> 00:08:03,883
Today, it is very different.

114
00:08:04,033 --> 00:08:06,768
In March 1989, a geomagnetic storm

115
00:08:06,848 --> 00:08:10,328
collapsed the power
grid in Quebec, Canada,

116
00:08:10,408 --> 00:08:13,971
in about ninety seconds,
leaving six million

117
00:08:14,051 --> 00:08:17,898
people without electricity for nine hours.

118
00:08:17,978 --> 00:08:23,405
In 2022, even a minor storm warmed
and expanded Earth's upper atmosphere,

119
00:08:23,485 --> 00:08:28,230
dragging dozens of newly
launched satellites out of orbit.

120
00:08:28,310 --> 00:08:33,698
Strong storms can disturb GPS navigation,
satellite communications,

121
00:08:33,778 --> 00:08:38,963
and the radio links used by
aircraft flying over the poles.

122
00:08:39,043 --> 00:08:41,457
Astronauts are at risk too.

123
00:08:41,607 --> 00:08:47,470
Outside the protection of Earth's magnetic
field, on the way to the Moon or Mars,

124
00:08:47,550 --> 00:08:50,552
a burst of solar particles could deliver a

125
00:08:50,632 --> 00:08:54,597
dangerous dose of radiation
in a matter of hours.

126
00:08:54,677 --> 00:08:57,679
And in July 2012,
an eruption as powerful as

127
00:08:57,759 --> 00:09:01,490
the Carrington event tore
through Earth's orbit.

128
00:09:01,570 --> 00:09:04,315
We missed it by about nine days.

129
00:09:06,665 --> 00:09:11,760
That is why a fleet of spacecraft
now watches the Sun around the clock.

130
00:09:11,840 --> 00:09:17,727
NASA's Solar Dynamics Observatory captures
the Sun in many wavelengths of light,

131
00:09:17,807 --> 00:09:21,384
recording a new image
every twelve seconds.

132
00:09:21,464 --> 00:09:24,722
The joint European and
American observatory

133
00:09:24,802 --> 00:09:27,361
SOHO has been watching since 1995,

134
00:09:27,441 --> 00:09:30,854
blocking the bright disk
of the Sun to reveal

135
00:09:30,934 --> 00:09:34,332
eruptions streaming through the corona.

136
00:09:34,412 --> 00:09:38,629
And NASA's Parker Solar Probe
has flown closer to the Sun than

137
00:09:38,709 --> 00:09:43,307
any spacecraft in history,
passing through the corona itself.

138
00:09:43,387 --> 00:09:48,022
At its closest, it races past
at around seven hundred thousand

139
00:09:48,102 --> 00:09:53,110
kilometers per hour, the fastest
object ever built by humans.

140
00:09:53,190 --> 00:09:58,969
The European Space Agency's Solar Orbiter,
built in partnership with NASA,

141
00:09:59,049 --> 00:10:02,452
has climbed out of the
plane of the planets,

142
00:10:02,532 --> 00:10:07,890
and in 2025 it gave us our first
ever views of the Sun's poles.

143
00:10:07,970 --> 00:10:12,102
The poles are where the Sun's
magnetic cycle may be decided.

144
00:10:12,182 --> 00:10:17,415
Closer to home, spacecraft parked
about one and a half million kilometers

145
00:10:17,495 --> 00:10:23,468
from Earth, between us and the Sun,
measure the solar wind before it arrives.

146
00:10:23,548 --> 00:10:26,539
They give forecasters
a warning of up to an

147
00:10:26,619 --> 00:10:30,417
hour before a storm's
magnetic cloud reaches us.

148
00:10:30,497 --> 00:10:33,652
That is enough time for
power companies to protect

149
00:10:33,732 --> 00:10:37,402
their grids and for satellite
operators to prepare.

150
00:10:37,482 --> 00:10:41,033
Space weather forecasters rate geomagnetic

151
00:10:41,113 --> 00:10:45,720
storms on a scale from G1,
minor, to G5, extreme.

152
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:49,440
Like weather forecasts on Earth,
their predictions are

153
00:10:49,520 --> 00:10:53,979
improving every year,
but the Sun can still surprise us.

154
00:10:55,829 --> 00:10:59,367
The Sun gives us light,
warmth, and life itself.

155
00:10:59,447 --> 00:11:05,671
But it is also a dynamic, violent star,
and we live inside its outer atmosphere.

156
00:11:05,751 --> 00:11:11,563
Every aurora that dances across the night
sky is a reminder of that connection.

157
00:11:11,643 --> 00:11:15,816
The next great solar storm is
not a question of if, but when.

158
00:11:15,896 --> 00:11:18,537
And thanks to the
spacecraft that keep watch

159
00:11:18,617 --> 00:11:21,939
on our restless star,
we will see it coming.
